<rss version="0.92"><channel><item>
            <title>[LU-20661] ptlrpc: improve the RPC PM QoS constraint</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20661</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-18446" title="Ensure CPUs are configured with the proper power management mode for optimal latency" class="issue-link" data-issue-key="LU-18446"&gt;&lt;del&gt;LU-18446&lt;/del&gt;&lt;/a&gt; (54a64ea818d9) added a PM QoS constraint to keep the CPUs of an RPC round trip out of deep C-states.  Two problems with it:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;1. It covers too few CPUs.  kick_cpu_latency() only lowers the latency of the cpt the primary NID maps to, while the thread consuming the reply runs elsewhere &amp;#8211; the caller for a sync RPC, a ptlrpcd for an async one.  A multi-rail peer reached over another interface maps to the wrong cpt as well, since only the primary NID is ever seen.  The wakeups form a chain, so whichever link is left in a deep C-state pays the exit latency anyway and most of the gain is lost.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;2. The tunables accept values that cannot work.  ptlrpc.pmqos_latency_max_usec and pmqos_default_duration_usec are stored with no range check: a negative latency is silently ignored by the PM QoS core, a duration that usecs_to_jiffies() rounds to 0 takes and drops the constraint on every RPC, and anything above UINT_MAX is truncated.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
